# What never gets uploaded to a deployment.
#
# Vercel's own defaults already exclude most of this. The file is explicit
# anyway, because a change in those defaults should not be able to quietly start
# shipping a local credential, and because being able to read the rule is worth
# more than trusting one.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- secrets
# Environment variables belong in the project settings, never in the upload.
# `.env.local` in particular holds the local Supabase stack's keys, including a
# service-role key that bypasses every row-level security policy.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# ------------------------------------------------- build output and caches
# The builder produces its own; uploading ours would only slow it down and
# risks a stale artifact being preferred to a fresh one.
.next
out
build
dist
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# ------------------------------------------------------------ dependencies
# The builder runs `npm ci` from package-lock.json. A node_modules copied from
# macOS would carry the wrong `sharp` binary for a Linux runtime.
node_modules

# ------------------------------------------------------ local tooling state
# Written by the Supabase CLI while a local stack runs. Nothing at runtime
# reads it, and it can contain connection details for the local containers.
supabase/.branches
supabase/.temp

# The migrations and the test suite are kept: they are part of the repository's
# documentation of the schema, they are small, and excluding them would make the
# deployed source a misleading copy of the project.
